  "tables": [
    {
      "table": "6.11",
      "position": 1,
      "ids": [
        "install.plugin"
      ],
      "cells": [
        "`install.plugin({ interactive, platform?, claude?, openai?, copilot?, opencodeZen?, zaiCodingPlan?, kimiForCoding?, opencodeGo?, bailianCodingPlan?, vercelAiGateway?, and the three further provider inputs of `InstallArgs`, codex_autonomous?, skip_auth })`. `claude` is `no`, `yes` or `max20`; every other provider input is `no` or `yes`; each is its own typed field. The registry holds no placeholder for them: it enumerates the twelve provider keys of `InstallArgs` (`C/types.ts:3-21`) mechanically, checks each against its Commander option (`C/cli-program.ts:57-76`) and its mapping (`C/install-command-options.ts:38-55`), freezes all twelve as rows in P0's baseline, and requires a scenario per key and value. This document's prose abbreviates three of the twelve names; the registry does not. `platform` is `opencode`, `codex`, `both`, `native` or `native-dev`; it defaults to `codex` when the binary is invoked as that edition, and `native-dev` is refused unless the release enables it (`C/install-command-options.ts:23-36`). `codex_autonomous` has three input cases, kept as they are today: `true` configures that platform's autonomous permissions; `false` leaves the existing permission settings unchanged (`C/cli-program.ts:74-76`); absent takes the mode's default, which in a non-interactive install for that platform is to configure them (`C/install-validators.ts:244`) and in an interactive one is the prompt's answer. Each case is a scenario, and the contract is one the security design reviews. The call answers what it installed and wrote per platform",
        "`install` (alias `setup`) `[--no-tui] [one flag per provider input] [--codex-autonomous \\| --no-codex-autonomous] [--skip-auth]`, with the root option `--platform`",
        "**no**",
        "Planned (OC1a). The interactive prompts are a renderer over the same call",
        "OC1a"
      ]
    },
